This summer my partner, Natalie, wrote(!) and performed in(!) an original musical called *[Island Dating Show](https://www.broadwayworld.com/off-off-broadway/article/ISLAND-DATING-SHOW-to-Open-at-Off-The-Lane-in-an-Interactive-Reality-TV-Musical-20260724)*, a parody of *Love Island*git.

If you have been living under a rock, *Love Island* is a reality dating show in which "hot bombshells move to a luxury villa in the hope of finding love and the summer of their life." Throughout each season, the at-home audience votes for their couples and islanders, ultimately choosing the couple that wins the show (and maybe more importantly, the cash prize).

It then followed that *Island Dating Show: The Musical*, must have live audience voting as well to ensure it stays as close to the source material as much as possible. And that's where I came in. Having only seen a couple of episodes of Love Island and in no way able to write a musical, I was not going to be much help on the content, but I sure can make a website where you click a button and something happens.

Before I get into the details of the project, a quick aside.

## Artists and engineers

Artists working with engineers have a long history that I have always been fascinated by. A few years ago, I saw an exhibit at the [Getty](https://www.getty.edu/), the best museum in Los Angeles, about a 1966 event called "[9 Evenings: Theatre & Engineering](https://news.artnet.com/art-world/experiments-art-technology-eat-getty-show-2555785).” This event brought together 10 artists in collaboration with 30 engineers from Bell Labs, intended to "showcase the possibilities of marrying their skills." The event was planned by a group called [Experiments in Art and Technology (E.A.T.)](https://www.experimentsinartandtechnology.org/) created by artists Robert Rauschenberg and Robert Whitman in collaboration with Bell Labs engineers Billy Klüver and Fred Waldhauer. 
